  "interpretation": {
    "primary_result": "Under the identical causal-information contract, the compact HayFlow voltage bridge has lower authentic one-step voltage RMSE than the 8,002-parameter Branch-ELM core for every paired seed.",
    "global_effect": "The all-segment advantage is consistent but moderate: 3.15%, 6.44%, and 9.96%, with a 6.44% median reduction.",
    "soma_effect": "The soma advantage is larger: 26.37% to 35.80%, with a 34.94% median reduction.",
    "active_effect": "On active examples HayFlow reduces RMSE by 7.20% to 12.59%, with an 8.92% median reduction.",
    "persistence_context": "HayFlow beats persistence globally and at the soma for every seed; Branch-ELM provides only a small global gain and is worse than persistence at the soma for two of three seeds.",
    "scope_limit": "This ranks only the authentic one-step voltage transition on train-derived disjoint development support. It is not a rollout, spike, fresh-test, or published-checkpoint comparison.",
    "parameter_caveat": "The active voltage paths are close but not exactly parameter matched: 8,985 trainable HayFlow voltage parameters versus 8,002 Branch-ELM parameters. The frozen 7,212-parameter state updater cannot affect the scored voltage output.",
    "scientific_conclusion": "The result supports the current causal and morphology-aware voltage representation over the small Branch-ELM core under equal information, but the absolute errors remain too large to establish a successful neuron surrogate."
  },
